RangeForce: Uncovering Open Permissions
Platform: RangeForce
Room: Uncovering Open Permissions
Difficulty: Advanced
Date: 2026
Objective
The objective of this lab was to identify and exploit misconfigured services and overly permissive access controls across a target environment. The focus was on discovering publicly accessible resources, weak configurations, and improperly secured services that could lead to unauthorized access and data exposure.
Step 1: Service Enumeration with Nmap
I began by performing network reconnaissance using Nmap to identify exposed services and potential entry points.
During the scan, I discovered:
Anonymous FTP login enabled
SMB service with guest access allowed
This highlighted common misconfigurations where services are exposed without proper authentication controls, significantly increasing the attack surface.
Step 2: Directory and Resource Discovery with Gobuster
Next, I used Gobuster to enumerate hidden directories and files on the target system.
Through this process, I discovered sensitive information, including:
MySQL credentials (root password) exposed in accessible directories
This demonstrated how improperly secured web directories can leak critical credentials, allowing attackers to escalate access within the system.
Step 3: Exploiting SMB Guest Access
With SMB guest access enabled, I was able to connect to shared resources without authentication.
This allowed:
Browsing of shared directories
Access to potentially sensitive internal files
This misconfiguration illustrates the risks of allowing unauthenticated access to network shares, which can lead to data leakage and further compromise.
Step 4: Public Cloud Storage Exposure
During the assessment, I identified publicly accessible cloud storage buckets.
Key findings included:
Read access to all stored data
Ability to enumerate bucket contents
This demonstrated a critical security issue where cloud resources were exposed without proper access restrictions, leading to full data disclosure.
Step 5: Impact Analysis
Combining all findings, the environment was highly vulnerable due to multiple layers of misconfiguration:
Weak service authentication (FTP, SMB)
Exposed credentials (MySQL root password)
Publicly accessible storage (cloud buckets)
An attacker could leverage these issues to:
Gain initial access via anonymous services
Escalate privileges using discovered credentials
Exfiltrate sensitive data from internal shares and cloud storage
Conclusion & Outcome
Through this lab, I gained hands-on experience identifying and exploiting open permissions across multiple services. This exercise reinforced how critical proper access control and secure configuration are in preventing unauthorized access.
It also highlighted how attackers chain multiple low-severity misconfigurations into a full compromise of an environment.
